the average car insurance premium could  increase by 75 pounds a year because of  a government ruling that will change the  formula for calculating personal injury  compensation payments currently courts  take two and a half percent from  compensation Awards to reflect the  interest a victim will earn on the lump  sum when they put it in the bank but  because the formula is based on the  interest rate on government bonds which  has now turned negative in real terms  that discount rate is to be cut to minus  point seven five percent meaning  insurers will now pay out significantly  more in lump sums the accountants at pwc  have calculated this could mean young  drivers see their annual premiums rise  by up to 1,000 pounds whereas older  drivers could see there's increase by  300 pounds while the association of  british insurers says the compensation  bill facing the NHS could go up by 1  billion pounds when use of the ruling  hammered the shares of the insurance  companies which say their profits will  be hit massively direct lion closed down  seven percent after it said its pre-tax  profits might be 230 million pounds  lower an admiral shares lost nearly  two-and-a-half percent after it said it  could lose as much as 175 million pounds  and that it was delaying publication of  its annual results Hugh Evans is  directed general of the association of  british insurers and joining us from  Oldham near Manchester is Neil Sugarman  he's president of the Association of  personal injury lawyers will kneel let  me start with you you campaigned for  this change how much of these extra  payouts are going to go into the hands  of personal injury lawyers as opposed to  real victims none none at all this is  all about injured people and we're  talking here specifically about very  seriously injured people who have  life-changing injuries and the point  about these claims is that they relate  to people who will probably never work  again and probably have to be careful  for the rest of their lives and that and  that's why we welcome this announcement  because it's they who are going to  benefit from the Lord Chancellor's  decision so none of your members will be  putting up their rates or anything like  that after these more generous payout  stock coming through the system most  certainly not this is nothing to do with  sisters fees there  predetermined in any event this is a  totally separate topic now given that  this discount rate is set in accordance  with gilt yields government UK  government bonds the latter have been  completely distorted by the bank of  england's asset purchase scheme isn't  this a rather silly way to proceed and  it's about the fact that people who've  been seriously injured tend to be very  risk-averse I've been in practice acting  on behalf of seriously injured people  for 35 years so I'm pretty well placed  to say that people do not want to take  risks with their compensation when they  know it's going to be very important to  their care and they can't readily go out  to obtain income to replace what they've  lost so that's what it's about and I  think we have to respect the fact that  there are vulnerable people and  therefore they have to be very careful  about what they do with the compensation  when it's earned and this is a  reflection of that it's a sensible  reflection of that well Hugh Evans that  that is a fair point i mean this rate  has been at the same level for 16 years  that was a real danger that some  accident victims would go in penniless  they were losing their pet their payouts  and we've never objected to the rate  being reviewed and indeed have supported  calls for the formula to be revised to  deliver a fairer rate we've taken part  in all the government consultations that  has had over many years on this subject  I agree with Neil it's vital that these  claimants get full and fair compensation  and it's very important that they have  lots of options to do so of course one  of the things they can now do which they  didn't used to be able to do is ask the  insurer to manage the money for them to  think something called a periodic  payment order and that is a perfect  option for a claimant who is worried  they may not be able to manage to put  the money properly which has not been  taken account of in the decision reached  today right now of course the government  has just clamped down on whiplash claims  which account for something like half of  all personal injury claims I'm in the  government's in effect take them with  one hand but they've given you back  something with the other haven't they  well I wish the two were evenly balanced  but the impact of today's decision  amounts to many billions of pounds  impacts not just for insurers but as  your introduction noted for the NHS  that's money that could come out of  front-line care and for the Ministry of  Defence and it the reason why this is a  flawed decision is it's based  formula that nobody actually uses none  of the claimants that Neil represents  invest in index little guilt if they do  it they will be losing money hand over  fist which is why Neil would never  advise them to invest in there surely  it's time we have a formula that's based  on how people actually invest in real  life all I knew whatever what do you  what do you make of what you've just  heard from Hugh Evans and I reiterate  that people who've received personal  injury compensation are cautious  investors they have to be and what's  been happening since 2001 is that  they've been in a situation where by  being cautious investors their money as  in some cases run out what does that do  it puts them back having to rely on the  National Health Service and local  authority funding well we all know the  situation in relation to public service  and the stretching of public services at  the moment so we say that the insurance  industry has had plenty of time to  prepare for what's going to happen we  think the approach is a sensible  approach and really with proper  improvement planning we probably  wouldn't be hearing the sort of noises  we're hearing from the insurance  industry today well there's no reason  for anyone's money to run out because I  said they can honestly insure us to  invest that money for them and that's  something that should have been taken  account in these trustees decision today  we all agree there should be a sensible  formula but it has to be one that is  based on how people actually invest  their money and I come back to the point  that this is that we have to have a  formula that reflects that and that  helps people manage their money over the  medium term and that compensates them  adequately there are perfectly  reasonable ways of doing this without  this decision which she's reached today  and to correct Neil we've not had lots  of warning about this this was announced  the stock market on the seventh of  December less than three months ago it's  been done during the closed period when  insurers are preparing for their results  thereby causing maximum chaos for the  sector and this after the Ministry of  Justice has spent the last five years  consulting on this issue without  publishing the responses to any of their  consultations so I don't think anyone  could argue this has been a well handled  process by the Ministry of Justice or  one that has provided certainty to  business I know this is a very important  point isn't I mean all of business right  now needs certainty and a stable  policymaking environment and the  insurance sector certainly hasn't  received that  the association of personal injury  lawyers has been calling on the Lord  Chancellor for six years to do something  about the discount rate 2001 it's 2001  that the rate was last looked at we say  the insurers have had plenty of time to  plan for this but we mustn't lose focus  we're talking about people who are  confined to wheelchairs we're talking  about people who've lost limbs we're  talking about people who have been  severely injured life changing  industries and I'm sorry that's where we  should be focusing not on big business  we are focusing on those we're actually  the people the industry who actually pay  out the claims that enable those people  to live the rest of their life with  dignity and properly cared for so  anything we need any lectures from the  other side about how those people should  be looked after and the importance of  making sure that their payouts matter  that's exactly the debate we've been  engaged in for the last five years but  equally if we're going to perform that  function and ensure for society as a  whole that we have affordable premiums  for motorists particularly for young  drivers who need affordable car  insurance so that they can work as well  as for older drivers to keep them on the  road as long as they wish to be on then  we have to have a balanced system based  on a fair formula and I struggle to see  how the personal injury lawyers can  defend a formula that none of them would  actually advise their members to rely on  what about Neil's points that the sector  has in effect enjoyed some form of  windfall gains during the last 16 years  by the fact that this rate has gone  unchanged for so long well the rate has  gone unchanged for a long time but  that's jus been doing a range of  economic circumstances I don't think  many of our members have enjoyed quite  the windfalls that Neil seems to imagine  but nonetheless we have contributed  through at that time to a constructive  debate about how this could be done  differently we're not people saying  never never don't change a thing we're  saying let's have a formula that's fair  that's balanced that takes account of  the need to provide affordable insurance  right across the economy that provides a  fair deal for claimants and that  critically is based on the way in which  those claimants actually invest their  money not some made-up metric that is 20  years old and that doesn't occupy place  in the real world anymore of course lest  we forget we're about to get an increase  in insurance premium tax from ten  percent to twelve percent is there an  argument for saying that should be put  on hold now while  the system digests these changes well  unfortunately the government put  insurance premium tax up three times in  the last 18 months from six percent to  twelve percent that together with the  increasing cost of car repairs as we're  all driving more expensive cars with  lots of electrics in them and the  increasing cost of whiplash these things  were already driving up motor premiums  before Liz trust his decision so there's  certainly a case for government to act  urgently and what we would like to see  them do is reform the law urgently to  produce the formula that I've argued for  done quickly and fairly so that we can  have a sensible balanced outcome for  everyone

